User Journey
A user types www.foobar.com in their browser.

The DNS system resolves www.foobar.com to the IP of the HAProxy load balancer cluster.

The load balancer distributes traffic across the backend servers.

Each backend server is specialized: one runs the web server (Nginx), one runs the application server, and one runs the database (MySQL).

The user receives the website content securely over TCP/IP with HTTPS.

🖥️ Additional Elements and Why They’re Added
1 extra server → Added to split responsibilities. Instead of all components running on one machine, each server handles a specific role (web, app, or database). This improves performance and makes scaling easier.

HAProxy load balancer cluster → Added for high availability. Two HAProxy instances work together so if one fails, the other continues distributing traffic. This removes the load balancer as a single point of failure.

Split components (web, app, DB) → Added to separate concerns:

Web server (Nginx) → Handles static content and HTTP requests.

Application server → Runs the business logic and dynamic code.

Database (MySQL) → Stores and organizes persistent data.

📌 Roles Explained
Web server (Nginx) → Manages incoming requests, serves static files, and forwards dynamic requests to the application server.

Application server → Executes the codebase, processes logic, and generates dynamic responses.

Database (MySQL) → Provides structured storage and query capabilities.

Load balancer cluster (HAProxy) → Distributes traffic using algorithms like Round Robin or Least Connections, ensuring balanced load and redundancy.

⚠️ Issues with This Infrastructure
SPOF reduced but not eliminated → The HAProxy cluster mitigates load balancer failure, but the database remains a single point of failure if only one instance accepts writes.

Complexity → Splitting components requires careful configuration and monitoring.

Scaling limits → While easier to scale than all‑in‑one servers, each tier must be scaled independently (e.g., adding more app servers or database replicas).

